27] \"Turn over a new leaf?\" [3228] \"Just an assumption. If you were Victor King, use your imagination. You just got out of prison, ready to start a new life, no longer with the thrill of adventure; you even met a beautiful girl and planned to settle down, marry, and retire. Live a quiet life from then on. Could you relate to that feeling?\" [3229] \"I wouldn't think so.\" Lemont's face showed a mocking smile. [3230] \"Maybe not,\" Anthony admitted, \"but you're not him?\" [3231] \"Your words are nonsense.\" [3232] \"Not at all nonsense, Lemont. Tell me, if I were the Vicomte, what crime could you accuse me of? The evidence of those old crimes is long gone. I've served my time, and it's wiped clean. You could try to find a French law based on 'loitering with intent to commit a crime,' but you'd still be unsatisfied, wouldn't you?\" [3233] \"You forgot,\" Lemont said. \"America! You impersonated the Prince Nicolas Paulvich, that's a crime.\" [3234] \"Useless, Lemont,\" Anthony said. \"At that time, I had nothing to do with America, and I could prove it without much effort. If the Vicomte had impersonated Prince Nicolas in America, then I wouldn't be the Vicomte. Are you sure it was an impersonation, and not the Prince Nicolas himself?\" [3235] Suddenly, Chief Constable Bettlebutt interjected, \"Mr. Kidd, that man is a fraud.\" [3236] \"Sheriff Batter, I won't argue with you,\" Anthony said. \"You're always right. So, you believe Prince Nicolas died in Congo?\" [3237] Sheriff Batter looked at him curiously. [3238] \"I'm not sure, but everyone says so.\" [3239] \"Careful with your words,\" Anthony said. \"What was your motto? 'Do not do evil, lest evil befall you.' I took a leaf out of your book and let Lemont say whatever he wanted, without denying it. But I'm afraid he'll be disappointed. You know I always have a trump card. I've brought one here, to be precise, a person, upstairs.\" [3240] \"Upstairs?\" said the Marquis de Carterham with interest. [3241] \"Yes, the poor fellow's been having a rough time lately, his head's been beaten pretty badly. I've been taking care of him.\" [3242] Suddenly, Mr. Issakov's deep voice came from the side: \"Can we guess who it is?\" [3243] \"Be my guest.\" Anthony said, \"But -\"\n[3244] Mr. Lemont interrupted him angrily:\n[3245] \"All nonsense! You think you're smarter than me. What you say might be true, you weren't in America at the time. If it weren't true, you wouldn't have said it. But there's another thing, a murder! Yes, a murder! Prince Michael's murder. That night when you were looking for the diamond, he ruined your scheme.\" [3246] \"Lemaire, have you heard of Victor King killing anyone?\" Anthony's voice was sharp. \"You know better than me, he never kills.\" [3247] \"Who else would kill him?\" Lemaire shouted. \"Tell me!\" [3248] As he finished speaking, a sharp whistle came from the corridor. Anthony put aside his previous indifference and jumped up. [3249] \"Didn't you ask me who killed Michael Prince?\" he called. \"I don't need to tell you, I'll show you. That whistle is the signal I've been waiting for. The murderer of Michael is now in the library.\" [3250] He jumped out of the window, followed by the others, circling the platform, and coming to the window of the library. He pushed it, and the window opened. [3251] He gently pulled the thick window curtain aside, and everyone could see the scene inside the room. [3252] A shadow stood in front of the bookshelf, intently pulling out one book after another and putting them back, completely oblivious to the noise outside. [3253] The figure held a flashlight, the dim light of which vaguely outlined his silhouette, and everyone stood there trying to recognize his appearance. Suddenly, someone jumped past them, letting out a roar like a wild beast. [3254] The flashlight fell to the ground and went out, and the room was filled with the sounds of terrifying struggle. The Earl of Carrington felt his way to the switch and turned on the light. [3255] Two people were wrestling, and when the crowd surrounded them, everything was over. A quick gunshot, and the short man bent over and fell to the ground. The other turned around to face everyone, it was Percy. His eyes were filled with anger. [3256] \"She killed my master,\" he roared, \"and now she wants to kill me. I took the gun from her and pointed it at her, but it went off during the struggle. It was the orders of the Duke of Michael, and this evil woman is dead.\" [3257] \"A woman?\" George Romax exclaimed. [3258] They approached the body, which lay on the floor, holding the gun in her hand, with a sinister expression on her face. It was Miss White. [3259] * * *\n[3260] [1] Alphonse Bertillon: A French Parisian clerk in the Identification Department in the 19th century; he developed the first modern system of criminal identification. This system, known as \"Bertillonage,\" consists of three parts: physical measurements, accurate descriptions of the physical characteristics of the suspect, and standardized facial photographs. [3261] Chapter 28: The Prince Victor\n[3262] \"I suspected her from the beginning,\" Anthony said, \"There was light in her room on the night of the murder. Later, I was unsure. I went to Bridesdale to investigate her, and when I returned, I believed she was Miss White. I was stupid at the time. Because Countess Bridesdale did indeed employ a Miss White and praised her highly. I never thought that the real Miss White was kidnapped on her way to her job and replaced by someone else. I even suspected Mr. Fiche. It was only when he came to Dover with me that we finally cleared the air. Then I began to see what was really going on. When I realized that he was from Pinkerton's and was tracking Prince Victor, my suspicions fell back on the original person.\" [3263] \"What most troubled me was that Mrs. Ruef recognized the woman, and it was only after I mentioned that the woman was the governess of Countess Bridesdale that Mrs. Ruef recognized her, and she only said that she felt the woman was familiar. Inspector Barton will soon explain to everyone that there was a carefully planned conspiracy to prevent Mrs. Ruef from coming to the Smokehouse. It was just a dead body after all. Although the person was killed by the Red Hand for betrayal, the planning of the entire action, as well as the absence of the Red Hand's mark, shows that there must have been a mastermind behind the scenes. At first, I thought it had something to do with Hesslov, as Mrs. Ruef was the only guest at the villa who had been to that country. I first suspected that someone was impersonating Prince Michael, but it turned out to be baseless. Then I thought Miss White might be an imposter, and when I remembered that Mrs. Ruef said she felt familiar with her, I could finally see through the clouds. It was clear that one thing was very important, that she could not be recognized by anyone, and Mrs. Ruef was the only one who might recognize her.\" [3264] \"Who is she?\" asked the Marquis of Cartonham.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1547] Anthony rowed the boat to the shore. Soon, they were reunited with the Earl of Carrington and his daughter. [1548] \"Lunch has been delayed,\" the Earl said dejectedly. [1549] \"I guess it's because Inspector Batterton spoiled the chef's mood.\" [1550] \"Lady Bander, this is my friend,\" Virginia said, \"Be nice to him.\" [1551] Bendl stared intently at Anthony for several minutes, as if he didn't exist, and then said to Virginia, \"Where do you find all these handsome men? How do you find them?\" she asked enviously. [1552] \"I'll give him to you,\" Virginia said generously. \"I want Lord Caterham.\" [1553] She smiled at the lord and took his arm. They left together. [1554] \"Can you talk?\" Bendl asked. \"Or do you have a hard shell and don't like to speak?\" [1555] \"Talk?\" Anthony said. \"I can chatter, grumble, and mutter. I can be quite verbose at times. Sometimes, I even ask questions.\" [1556] \"For example, what?\" [1557] \"Who lives in the second room from the left?\" he said, pointing to the room. [1558] \"What a strange question!\" Bandel said, \"You're such a funny person. Let me think... Ah, that's Miss White's room, the French governess. She tries to discipline my two sisters, Daisy and Desi, it sounds like a song. If there were another child, I think it would be called Delecey Me. But Mother got tired of always having girls, so she died, hoping that someone else could give Father a male heir.\" [1559] \"Miss White,\" Anthony said thoughtfully, \"How long has she been here?\" [1560] \"Two months, she came when we were in Scotland.\" [1561] \"Ha!\" Anthony said. \"I smell a whiff of treachery.\" [1562] \"I hope I smell the smell of lunch,\" Bunder said. \"Mr. Ked, should we invite the police to join us for lunch? You're a worldly person, you know the etiquette of such things. We've never had a murder in the house. It's too exciting. Only this morning you were cleared of suspicion. I've always wanted to meet a murderer, to see if they're as nice and charming as they say in the Sunday paper. What's that?\" [1563] The \"that\" in Bunder's mouth was a taxi driving towards the villa, with two passengers inside. One was tall, bald, with a black beard; the other was shorter and looked younger, with a black mustache. Anthony recognized the tall man immediately. He guessed that it was this man who had frightened his companion, not the taxi. [1564] \"If I'm not mistaken,\" he said, \"that's my old friend, Baron Lolopraitcher.\" [1565] \"What baron?\" [1566] \"For convenience, I call him Lolly. His full name is too difficult to pronounce, it would give me an aneurysm.\" [1567] \"The phone has been ringing off the hook this morning,\" said Bunter. \"It's definitely Lolly. I can feel him coming this afternoon. I've been dealing with the Eisakistanis all morning. Let George handle his politics. I'm sorry, Sir Cedric, but I must go and see my poor father.\" [1568] Bunter rushed back to the house. [1569] Anthony watched her retreating figure for a few minutes, then, pondering, lit a cigarette. Suddenly, he heard a furtive sound, very close by. He was standing by the boathouse, and the sound seemed to be coming from around the corner, as if someone was trying unsuccessfully to stifle a sneeze. [1570] \"Whoever is hiding behind the boathouse,\" thought Anthony, \"I must investigate.\" [1571] He tossed the just-blown-out match and crept around the corner of the boathouse. [1572] There was a man struggling to get up, having been on his knees for a while. He was tall, wore glasses, and had a short, pointed black beard. He was dressed in a light-colored coat and had a somewhat effete air about him. He appeared to be between 30 and 40 years old. Overall, he looked quite presentable. [1573] \"What are you doing here?\" Anthony asked. [1574] He was certain that the man was not a guest of the Earl of Carrington. [1575] \"I'm sorry,\" the stranger said with a distinct foreign accent, a charming smile on his face. \"I was trying to find my way back to the Happy Wanderer Inn, but I got lost. Could you tell me how to get there, sir?\" [1576] \"Of course,\" Anthony said. \"But you must know, you can't take a boat over there.\" [1577] \"What?\" The stranger looked a bit bewildered. [1578] \"I mean,\" Anthony repeated, glancing intentionally at the boathouse, \"you can't take a boat over there. You can walk through the yard, and it's a bit of a distance, but it's a private residence, and you're trespassing now.\" [1579] \"I'm sorry,\" the stranger said. \"I got completely lost and was just looking for directions.\" [1580] Anthony wanted to point out that asking for directions in the boathouse was a bit odd, but he restrained himself. He warmly took the stranger's arm. [1581] \"You take this path,\" he said, \"and walk along the lake edge until you come to that little path. You'll find it. Once you're on that path, turn left, and you'll be in the village. Are you staying at the Happy Cricketer's Inn?\" [1582] \"Yes, sir,\" the stranger said. \"I checked in this morning. Thank you for your help with directions.\" [1583] \"You're welcome,\" Anthony said. \"I hope you haven't caught a chill.\" [1584] \"What?\" The stranger said. [1585] \"Kneeling on the damp ground can cause a chill,\" Anthony explained. \"I just heard you sneeze.\" [1586] \"Maybe,\" the man admitted. [1587] \"Indeed,\" Anthony said. \"But when you sneeze, don't suppress it. A famous doctor once told me that it's dangerous. I can't remember the exact harm it could cause, something like suppressing the central nervous system or causing vascular hardening. Anyway, don't do that anymore. Good morning!\" [1588] \"Good morning, and thank you again for your directions.\" [1589] \"Another suspicious stranger from the second village inn,\" Anthony thought as he watched the man leave. \"I don't have a good feeling about this person either. He looks like a French merchant traveling abroad. He doesn't seem like a member of the Red Hand Party. Could he be a third party in the Hessian uprising? The second window's tenant is a French family tutor, and now another mysterious Frenchman has come along, eavesdropping stealthily. I'm sure there's something going on here.\" [1590] As Anthony pondered, he walked back to the villa. He met the Duke of Carringham in the corridor, accompanied by two new guests who looked displeased. Seeing Anthony, their mood improved slightly. [1591] \"There you are,\" he said. \"Anthony Kidd, let me introduce you to\u2014the baron...uh...and Captain Andreas.\" [1592] The baron stared intently at Anthony, his suspicion growing heavier. [1593] \"Mr. Kidd?\" he said stiffly. [1594] \"Baron, may I speak with you privately?\" Anthony said. \"I will explain everything.\" [1595] The baron bowed, and the other two walked down the platform together. [1596] \"Baron,\" Anthony said, \"first, I must ask for your forgiveness. I abused the honor of a British gentleman by coming to this country under a false name. When I met you, I introduced myself as James McGlashan, but you should understand that such deception is inconsequential. You must know Shakespeare's works well, and he also said that the terms of a rose are irrelevant, right? The same applies here. You wanted to meet the person who wrote the memoir, and I am that person. And you also know that the memoir is no longer in my possession. Clever trick, very clever. Was it your idea or your superiors?\" [1597] \"It was the Prince's idea. And he insisted on doing it himself without anyone else's help.\" [1598] \"He did a good job,\" Anthony said approvingly, \"I always thought he was British.\" [1599] \"The Prince was taught by a British teacher. It's a tradition in Hesse.\" [1600] \"His methods of handling the manuscript were as good as a professional thief,\" Anthony said, \"Excuse me, but how are the manuscripts now?\" [1601] \"It was a gentleman's agreement not to tell anyone,\" said the baron. [1602] \"Baron, you are too kind,\" Anthony muttered. \"I have been called a gentleman more times in the last forty-eight hours than in my entire life.\" [1603] \"All I can tell you is that I guess it has been burned.\" [1604] \"That's just a guess, without any concrete evidence, right?\" [1605] \"The Prince kept the memoirs himself. His intention was to read them and then burn them.\" [1606] \"I see.\" Anthony said, \"But that manuscript isn't something you can read in half an hour. It's not light reading.\" [1607] \"My superior has already sacrificed for the country, and there was no manuscript found in his belongings. It's clear that it has been burned.\" [1608] \"Hmm!\" Anthony said, \"Maybe not.\" [1609] He fell silent for a couple of minutes, then continued, \"Baron