ounterpart of some shady dealings and has sent a telegram to the French authorities requesting his fingerprints and other identification.\" [3310] \"Ah!\" exclaimed the baron, \"fingerprints! And the scoundrel also mentioned the Beddington body-measuring table, didn't he?\" [3311] \"He's quite the clever one, isn't he?\" Anthony said. \"I had to give him some encouragement to fan the flames, and that way, I could confuse Lemont as much as possible. Whenever I mentioned 'the row of things' and the hiding place of the jewels, he eagerly relayed the information to his accomplices. On the other hand, he kept us all in the room. The note was for Miss White. He sent Tredwell to deliver it immediately, and Tredwell did just that, sending it to the classroom upstairs. While Lemont accused me of being King Viktor to distract us, nobody would leave the room. By the time everything was explained, the gem was already gone.\" [3312] George cleared his throat. [3313] \"Mr. Kidd, I must tell you, your actions in this matter are to be condemned,\" he said pompously. \"If there had been even the slightest flaw in your plan, we would have lost a national treasure and had no hope of recovering it. Mr. Kidd, you were extremely reckless and inexcusable.\" [3314] \"Mr. R\u00f6mer, I don't think you understand this idea yet,\" Fuchs said, drawing out his words. \"The historical artifact was not hidden behind the books in the library.\" [3315] \"Not there?\" [3316] \"Absolutely not.\" [3317] \"Actually,\" Anthony explained, \"Stapleton's little trinket represents its true meaning: the rose. On Monday afternoon, I thought of this and immediately went to the rose garden. Mr. Fitch and I thought of the same thing. Facing the sundial, walking seven steps forward, then eight steps to the left, and three steps to the right, you would arrive at the red rose bush, whose name is Richmond. The entire villa has been searched, but no one thought to look in the garden. We can organize a small excavation team tomorrow morning.\" [3318] \"So, the story about the book in the library\u2026\"\n[3319] \"It was a fabrication to lure Miss White into a trap. Mr. Fitch was watching from the balcony, waiting for the right moment to blow a whistle. I can tell you that I and Mr. Fitch set up a military cordon in the house in Dover to prevent the Red Hand from contacting the imposter Lemmon. He gave the order for them to leave, and they reported that they had done so. This made him happy to proceed with his plan and openly accuse me.\" [3320] \"Well, well,\" said the Earl of Carringham, \"Now everything is clarified, and we have a satisfactory result.\" [3321] \"There's one more thing,\" said the Duke of Austria. [3322] \"What is it?\" [3323] The financier looked steadily at Anthony. [3324] \"Why do you insist on bringing me here? Just to be a spectator and warm up the scene?\" [3325] Anthony shook his head. [3326] \"No, Mr. Esakistan. You're a busy man, your time is money. What brought you here?\" [3327] \"To discuss a loan.\" [3328] \"With whom?\" [3329] \"Prince Michael of Hesse.\" [3330] \"Exactly. Prince Michael is dead, so you're planning to provide the same terms to his cousin, Prince Nikolai?\" [3331] \"Can you introduce me to him? I thought he was killed in Congo.\" [3332] \"He was, by me. Not that I'm a murderer, I mean, I spread the news of his death. Mr. Isakov, I promise you a prince. How about 'I'? \" [3333] \"You?\" [3334] \"Yes, me. Nikolai Szejewsky Ferdinand Opolovitch. That name is too long for the life I want to lead, so I simply called myself Anthony Kade in Congo.\" [3335] Captain Andrusov was startled. [3336] \"Incredible, too unbelievable.\" He blurted out. [3337] \"Sir, you must be careful with your words.\" [3338] \"I have a lot of evidence,\" Anthony said calmly, \"I think I can convince the baron.\" [3339] The baron raised his hand. [3340] \"I will certainly check your evidence, but I don't need them. Just your words are enough. And you look so much