e tonight.\" [3124] Isakov hesitated, looking at him uncertainly. [3125]\n[3126] \"You suggest I do that?\" [3127] Anthony leaned in close to Isaacstan's ear and whispered a few words. The financial expert was stunned, staring at him in disbelief. [3128] \"Are you really going to\u2026\"\n[3129] \"You'll see,\" Anthony said. [3130] Chapter Twenty-seven, October Thirteen (Continued)\n[3131] The clock in the council chamber struck nine. [3132] \"Ah,\" the Earl of Carrington sighed deeply, \"they're back, like sheep, wagging their tails one after the other, all back.\" [3133] He looked sadly around the room. [3134] \"A street singer with a monkey,\" he muttered, staring at the baron, \"and a busybody from Shiregrove Street\u2026\"\n[3135] \"That's not very friendly to say about the baron,\" Bunder protested after hearing his complaints. \"He told me you were the perfect embodiment of the British aristocracy's hospitality.\" [3136] \"I think,\u201d said the Earl of Chatham, \"he says that to everyone, without getting tired. But I'm no longer the hospitable British gentleman I used to be. Once I sell the chimney house to an American entrepreneur, I'll move to a hotel. There, if someone bothers you, you can settle the bill and leave right away.\" [3137] \"Cheer up,\u201d said Bunder, \"we may never see Mr. Fischer again.\" [3138] \"I always found him an interesting person,\u201d said the Earl of Chatham, his mood fluctuating, \"it's all your fault, you young people, for making me get involved in these things. Why did they have to hold their board meeting in my house? Why couldn't they go to Rousham, Hume Castle, or find a good villa in Stretton to hold their meeting?\" [3139] \"The atmosphere isn't right there,\u201d said Bunder.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe was expelled from Oxford University for misbehavior. Some say he died in Congo two years later, but it's just a rumor. He reappeared a few months ago when there were rumors of the monarchists plotting a comeback.\" [1408] \"Really?\" said the chief of police. \"Where did he appear?\" [1409] \"In America.\" [1410] \"America!\" [1411] Balthazar turned to Isakovsky and asked tersely, \"Oil?\" [1412] The big financier nodded. [1413] \"What he means is that, as a candidate for the throne of Hesse-Slav, he would be more popular than Prince Michael, because of his modern and enlightened ideas, and his early support for democratic and republican ideals. If the American consortium could support him, he would grant them a concession in return.\" [1414] At this point, Balthazar had forgotten his habit of keeping his emotions in check and whistled a long, low whistle. [1415] \"So that's it,\" he murmured. \"On the other hand, the monarchists support Prince Michael. But you think you have the upper hand. And then this happened.\" [1416] \"You don't think\u2026\" George said. [1417] \"It's a big deal,\" the sheriff said. \"That's what Mr. Isakov said. A big deal, must be a very big deal.\" [1418] \"There will always be underhanded situations,\" Isakov said calmly. \"Now, Wall Street has the upper hand. But we haven't settled our differences yet. Sheriff, if you want to serve your country, find the killer of Prince Michael.\" [1419] \"There's one thing that strikes me as suspicious,\" George interjected. \"Why didn't Captain Androidsi come with the prince yesterday?\" [1420] \"I've already looked into it,\" Balthazar said. \"It's quite simple. He stayed in the city to meet a young lady for Prince Michael to arrange a date for the following weekend. The baron disapproves of this, believing it to be unwise at this stage. So the prince had to do it secretly. He, you could say, is quite a free-spirited young man.\" [1421] \"Probably,\" said George stiffly. \"Ah, probably.\" [1422] \"There's one more thing we need to keep 