eeting room. \"The second one from that side?\" [1512] \"No, my room is on the other side, over by Elizabeth's side hall. What's going on?\" [1513] \"Last night, I thought I heard gunshots and went back, and the lights in that room turned on.\" [1514] \"That's strange! I don't know who's staying there, but I can ask Bunder. They might have heard the gunshots too.\" [1515] \"If so, they haven't said anything about it today. I heard from Inspector Bartlett that no one in the house heard gunshots. That's my only lead, not very reliable, but I'm still going to pursue it.\" [1516] \"It's really strange,\" Virginia pondered. [1517] They walked to the boathouse by the lake, leaning against the wall and talking. [1518] \"Let's go over everything now,\" Anthony said, \"let's take our time and row out on the lake so that the police, the American guests, and the gossiping maid can't overhear us.\" [1519] \"I heard some things from the Earl of Carringham,\" Virginia said, \"but not much. Tell me, are you Anthony Kidd or Jimmy McGregor?\" [1520] So, Anthony recounted the story he had told earlier, but with a slight difference in what he said to Virginia. From six weeks ago until he unexpectedly recognized the \"Mr. Holmes\" figure. [1521] \"By the way, Mrs. Rue,\" he said finally. \"I haven't thanked you yet for lying about me being your old friend.\" [1522] \"You are my old friend, aren't you?\" Virginia exclaimed. \"You don't think I'd have pushed the body on you and then pretend we're just acquaintances when we meet again, do you? I'm not that kind of person.\" She paused and then continued, \"You know, there's one thing that really surprises me. I had no idea that the memoir had such an unexpected mystery.\" [1523] \"You're right,\" Anthony agreed. \"There's one thing I want to ask you.\" [1524] \"What is it?\" [1525] \"When I mentioned Jimmy McGregor's name to you yesterday on Pound Street, why did you seem so surprised? Had you heard of him before?\" [1526] \"Sherlock Holmes, I have heard of him. George, my cousin George Roxborough, came to me one day and talked about a bunch of nonsense. He wanted me to come here and try to seduce this McGregor into giving up the memoir. Of course, he didn't say it like that. He talked about British ladies and all that useless stuff, but his intentions were clear. It's only poor old George who could come up with such a stupid idea. Later, I asked him a few more questions, and he started to lie to me. His lies were so bad that even a two-year-old child couldn't be fooled.\" [1527] \"Anyway, his plan seems to have succeeded,\" Anthony said. \"I came here, just as he wanted me to be Jimmy McGregor, and you were just what he wanted.\" [1528] \"But there's no memoir for poor old George! Now I have a question for you. When I said those letters weren't written by me, you said you knew. How did you know that?\" [1529] \"I do know,\" Anthony said with a smile. \"My psychological knowledge is quite solid.\" [1530] \"You mean you have confidence in my character, so...\"\n[1531] Anthony shook his head vigorously. [1532] \"Not at all. I know nothing about your character. You might have a lover and write him letters. But you wouldn't give in to blackmail. The real Virginia Ruff was terrified, but you held out.\" [1533] \"I'm curious about who the real 'Virginia Ruff' is, I mean where she is. It makes me feel like I have a doppelganger.\" [1534] Anthony lit a cigarette. [1535] \"Did you know that one of the letters was mailed from the Chimney House?\" he asked. [1536] \"What?\" Virginia was startled, \"When was the letter?\" [1537] \"There was no date on the letter, but it was quite strange.\" [1538] \"I'm sure there's no other Virginia Ruffin who has stayed at the Chimney House besides me, and if there was, the Earl of Carringham or Bunder would have told me about the coincidence of the name.\" [1539] \"Hmm, that's quite eerie. Mrs. Ruffin, I'm starting to deeply doubt the existence of the other Virginia Ruffin.\