ill was exhausted from George's demands. He had no opportunity to speak to Virginia all day, which left him feeling abused and hurt. Fortunately, the colonial had left. But still, he had taken up too much of Virginia's social time. If George Romax continued to toy with him like this\u2026 Bill went to sleep with a grudge, finding consolation in his dream where he dreamed of Virginia. [1908] In his dream, he played the role of a brave rescuer in a burning wood setting. He carried the unconscious Virginia down from the highest floor, placing her on the grass, and then ran off to find a box of sandwiches. The box of sandwiches was crucial, and he had to find it. George found it, but refused to give it to Bill, instead dictating an electric telegram to him. They were in the church's vestry, and Virginia could come over to marry him at any moment. There was a terrible thing! He was still wearing his pajamas, and he had to get home to change into proper clothes. He dashed out to the car, but it wouldn't start. The gas tank was empty! He felt incredibly desperate. Later, a generic public bus pulled up, and Virginia got off, with her arm around a balding baron. She was wearing a tasteful gray dress and looked beautiful. She walked over to him, playfully shaking his shoulder. \"Bill,\" she said, \"wake up, Bill.\u201d She shook him harder, \"Bill,\" she said, \"wake up, wake up!\u201d\n[1909] Bill awoke with a dizzying sensation. He was in his room at the chimney house. [1910] He was still not fully awake from his dream. [1911] Virginia was leaning over him, shaking him and repeating the same words. [1912] \"Wake up, Bill. Wake up, Bill!\" [1913] \"Virginia!\" Bill sat up. \"What's going on?\" [1914] Virginia breathed a sigh of relief. [1915] \"Thank goodness you're awake. I've been shaking you, trying to get you to wake up. Are you fully awake now?\" [1916] \"I should be,\" Bill said uncertainly. [1917] \"You big lug,\" Virginia said, \"it took so much effort to shake you, 