" [2306] \"As I said before, 'many wrongs don't make a right.'\" [2307] \"Besides,\" Anthony said, \"I'm still just an amateur assistant?\" [2308] \"That's right.\" [2309] \"Your Watson to my Sherlock Holmes?\" [2310] \"Most detective novels are just made-up nonsense,\" Battert said expressionlessly, \"but they're entertaining to read. Sometimes, they can be useful too.\" [2311] \"How so?\" asked Anthony curiously. [2312] \"They make the reader generally think the police are fools. That's useful when you come across an amateurish crime, like a murder, for instance.\" [2313] Anthony looked at him silently for a few minutes, Batterton sitting motionless, occasionally blinking, with no expression on his placid face. After a while, he got up. [2314] \"There's no use going to bed now,\" he said. \"I'll have to talk to the Earl when he gets up. Everyone can leave if they want to, but if the Earl can invite the guests to stay on a personal basis, I'll be grateful. Sir, if you don't mind, please accept his invitation, and Mrs. Ruff.\" [2315] \"Did you find the gun?\" Anthony asked suddenly. [2316] \"Do you mean the gun that killed Prince Michael? No, not yet. However, it's definitely inside the villa or in the villa's courtyard. I got an idea from you and sent a few people to search the bird's nest. If we can get that gun, the case will have some leads. And that bundle of letters. You said there was a letter with the heading 'Smokehouse Villa', right? That must be his last letter, and it will definitely mention how to find the diamond.\" [2317] \"What do you think about Gisipp's death?\" [2318] \"He was a habitual thief and was probably found by either the Vicomte or the Red Hand, and then hired. It's not surprising if the Vicomte and the Red Hand work together. The Red Hand has money and power, but lacks wisdom. Gisipp's task was to steal the memoir, and they couldn't know that the letters were in your possession. By the way, that's quite a strange coincidence.\" [2319] \"I know,\" Anthony said. \"It's really quite amazing to think about it.\" [2320] \"Gisipp got those letters instead, and he was initially disappointed. After seeing the fragment of the letter cut out, he came up with the idea of blackmailing the lady. Of course, he didn't realize the true value of those letters. The Red Hand's comrades discovered his actions and thought he had deliberately betrayed them, so they killed him. They are fond of executing traitors, which is a unique characteristic for them. What I don't understand is the gun with the inscription 'Virginia'. Such a clever handling is not something the Red Hand could come up with. Normally, they like to post their red hand marks everywhere to serve as a warning to others. I think the Vicomte must have gotten involved here, but I don't understand his motives. It seems he wanted to frame Mrs. Ruff, but there's no special significance in doing so.\" [2321] \"I had an idea,\" Anthony said, \"but it didn't work as planned.\" [2322] He confessed to Batter that he had identified Virginia as Michael, and the police chief nodded. [2323] \"It's definitely Michael. By the way, the old baron was very impressed with you when you mentioned you would do your best to find the memoir by next Wednesday.\" [2324] \"He's a good man,\" Anthony said. \"I appreciate his kind words, especially since I told him I would do my best to find the memoir by next Wednesday.\" [2325] \"That's not an easy task,\" Batter said. [2326] \"Well, you think so too? I guess the Victorians have gotten their hands on those letters.\" [2327] Butler nodded. [2328] \"Taking the letters from Giuseppe on Pound Street was all part of the plan. They have them now and have deciphered the code, so they know where to look.\" [2329] The two were about to leave the room. [2330] \"Here?\" Anthony turned sharply. [2331] \"Yes, right here. But they haven't found them yet, so they'll take another risk to come back.\" [2332] \"I guess,\" Anthony said, \"you've already come up with a plan in that clever head of yours?\" [2333] Bartle didn't answer, looking particularly slow and dull-witted. Then, he slowly blinked. [2334] \"Do you need my help?\" Anthony asked. [2335] \"Yes, and another person's help.\" [2336] \"Who?\" [2337] \"Mrs. Reeve. You should know, she's a particularly deceptive woman.\" [2338] \"I've already figured that out,\" Anthony said. [2339] He glanced at his watch. [2340] \"Bartle, I agree with you. Go for a swim in the lake and have a hearty breakfast instead of sleeping. It's more meaningful.\" [2341] He lightly ran back to the room upstairs, whistling and shedding his nightclothes. Then he took out a morning robe and a towel. [2342] Suddenly, he stopped in front of the vanity, staring intently at something quietly placed in front of the mirror. [2343] For a moment, he couldn't believe his eyes, he picked up the item and examined it carefully. Yes, that's right. [2344] It was the bundle of letters signed by Virginia Reveaux, intact and unopened. [2345] Anthony held those letters, sinking into the chair. [2346] \"I can't make sense of it,\" he muttered to himself. \"I don't understand what's happening in this villa. How did these letters come back? Who put them on my vanity? Why?\" [2347] He couldn't find satisfactory answers to these crucial questions. [2348] Chapter 21: Mr. Eschaton's Suitcase\n[2349] At 10 a.m., the Earl of Chatham and his daughter were having breakfast, and Bandel looked worried. [2350] \"Father,\" she finally spoke. [2351] The Earl of Carrington was engrossed in reading The Times, and did not speak. [2352] \"Father,\" Bandel called out again, more sharply. [2353] The Earl of Carrington was engrossed in reading the news of an upcoming rare book auction, and only lifted his head when she called out to him. [2354] \"Ah?\" he said, \"Did you say something?\" [2355] \"Yes, who was that person eating breakfast over there?\" [2356] She indicated the clearly occupied seat with a nod of her head. All the other seats were empty. [2357] \"Ah, what was his name again?\" [2358] \"Fat Eddi?\" [2359] Bendlar and her father had a remarkable ability to understand each other without words, and they both seemed to grasp the meaning of the puzzling words. [2360] \"Yes, that's the one.\" [2361] \"I saw you talking to the sheriff before breakfast.\" [2362] The Earl of Carrington sighed. [2363] \"Yes, he caught me in the foyer. In my opinion, the time before breakfast is sacred. I have to go abroad; the pressure here is too much.\" [2364] Bandel abruptly interrupted him. [2365] \"What did he say?\" [2366] \"He said that anyone who wants to leave can leave.\" [2367] \"Oh,\" Bandel said, \"that's just what you wanted.\" [2368] \"I know. But he didn't finish. He went on to say that he still hopes I can invite everyone to stay.\" [2369] \"I don't understand,\" Bandel said, frowning. [2370] \"It's all so confusing, so contradictory,\" the Earl of Carrington complained, \"and he had to say it before breakfast.\" [2371] \"What did you say?\" [2372] \"Of course I agreed. There's no point arguing with this group, especially before breakfast.\" The Duke of Cambridge returned to his most vexing question. [2373] \"So who did you invite?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."